\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Coordinates for conditional propagators}
\date{2026-11-10}

\p{Choose orthonormal basis maps #{B:\mathbb R^r\to X} and
#{B_\perp:\mathbb R^{n-r}\to Y} as in the proof of
\ref{fdip-0060}. In these bases the blocks are
#{A=B^TQB}, #{K=B^TQB_\perp} and #{D=B_\perp^TQB_\perp}, the
conditional mean coordinates are #{\mu(x)=-D^{-1}K^Tx}, and the
full noise covariance has matrix #{C=B_\perp D^{-1}B_\perp^T}.
For a coordinate selector retaining #{R} and eliminating
#{U=V\setminus R}, the same formulas are
##{\mu(x)=-Q_{UU}^{-1}Q_{UR}x,\qquad
   \operatorname{Cov}(\eta)=Q_{UU}^{-1}.}}

\p{In full vertex coordinates, with the symmetric four-tensor
#{T_{ijkl}} and #{h=h(x)}, Wick's three pairings give
##{\begin{aligned}
24a(x)={}&T(h,h,h,h)
 +6\sum_{i,j,k,l}T_{ijkl}h_i h_j C_{kl}\\
 &+3\sum_{i,j,k,l}T_{ijkl}C_{ij}C_{kl}.
\end{aligned}}
The covariance #{C} can be singular on #{H}: it is supported on
#{Y}. The formula follows by applying Wick to the nonsingular
#{D^{-1}} in eliminated coordinates. No nondegeneracy on all of
#{H} is needed.}

\p{TauCeti's
[\code{integral_mul_mul_mul_multivariateGaussian}](https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/df319cee080d8cab30699ff8ab3ed8d9347768aa/TauCeti/Probability/Distributions/Gaussian/MixedMoments.lean#L162)
proves the three-pairing identity for 4 centered Gaussian
coordinates, allowing repeated indices and any positive-semidefinite
covariance. Setting that covariance to #{C} supplies the fourth-moment
step. Contraction with #{T}, the formal logarithm and the
higher-moment pairing arguments are proved in the proposition.}

\p{TauCeti's
[\code{condDistrib_multivariateGaussian}](https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/blob/df319cee080d8cab30699ff8ab3ed8d9347768aa/TauCeti/Probability/Distributions/Gaussian/Conditional.lean#L444)
assumes a positive-semidefinite joint covariance and a
positive-definite observed covariance block. It identifies the
regular conditional law with an explicit Gaussian kernel almost
everywhere in the observed marginal. Order the coordinates as
#{Y\oplus X} and use #{G=Q^{-1}>0}. From #{QG=\bbI},
##{\begin{aligned}
DG_{YX}+K^TG_{XX}&=0,\\
DG_{YY}+K^TG_{XY}&=\bbI.
\end{aligned}}
Therefore #{G_{YX}G_{XX}^{-1}=-D^{-1}K^T} and
#{G_{YY}-G_{YX}G_{XX}^{-1}G_{XY}=D^{-1}}. This is the coordinate
form of the mean and covariance in the proposition. Its
almost-everywhere identification is distinct from the explicit
density kernel used there at every #{x}.}
